Final Project


Introduction

Preventative measures against adversarial attacks within a network can be difficult to
implement without knowledge of how these attacks begin and how they traverse through the
system. Our project aims to fill this knowledge gap by administering a controlled attack against a
network and implementing an intrusion detection system.
Relevant Work
Yersinia is a Kali Linux tool used to exploit vulnerabilities within the datalink layer’s
protocols. It can launch various attacks such as DoS and MitM against these protocols.
Yersinia’s easy deployment makes it a great tool for administering the attack.
One of the protocols Yersinia attacks within the datalink layer is the Dynamic Host
Configuration Protocol, also known as DHCP. DHCP is responsible for leasing IP addresses to
devices as they connect to a network. This protocol is commonly used for wireless LANs that
use Wi-Fi where devices frequently connect or disconnect from the network, as it removes the
time-consuming manual configuration of a new IP address every time a device connects.
Yersinia performs a DHCP starvation attack where it outputs requests for IPs from a single point
that spoofs fake MAC addresses. This attack exhausts the server’s pool of available IP addresses
and makes it so new devices cannot connect to the network.

Description

On our current small scale, the network would be local to one device and built using
multiple virtual machines running on Kali Linux. One virtual machine would work as the
network’s DHCP server, one serves as an attacker, and another as the client. The DHCP server
was configured using isc-dhcp-sever and sets up an internal network on the eth1 interface to
interact with the other virtual machines. Its set capability is to provide ~190 IP addresses on a
default lease of 600 seconds. Using virtual machines to create our network ensures safety for our
real devices and public networks.
To monitor the network during controlled attack, we have built an intrusion detection
system that alerts administrative users to irregular network activity. Our IDS operates at the
network layer and observes DHCP traffic across the local network. Because DHCP Discover
messages are broadcast to devices on the LAN, this provides a reliable vantage point for
detecting anomalies.
The IDS is implemented in Bash and uses ‘tcpdump’ to sniff UDP packets on ports 67
and 68, where DHCP usually places communications. Every DISCOVER message seen on the
network increments an internal counter. The script processes packets in one-second sampling
windows and evaluates the total number of Discover requests over ten second intervals. The
sliding window approach allows IDS to see the difference between normal network activity and
the attack caused by an external machine.
At the end of each ten second window, the IDS compares the accumulated Discover
count to a predefined threshold. If the count exceeds this limit, the script raises an alert indicating
that abnormal DHCP activity has been detected. This gives us clear and fast feedback about
possible attacks going on without interrupting the network or requiring any specialized hardware.
By relying on simple command line tools and transparent logic, our IDS is easy to modify
and/or extend. The threshold can be adjusted depending on the network’s expected load, and
additional detection rules can be added to monitor other DHCP messages or maybe even
additional protocols. But even if it is lightweight, this system effectively demonstrates how IDS
mechanisms can flag malicious behavior during DHCP based attacks.

Conclusions & Future Work
A large part of why DHCP is vulnerable to these attacks comes to its lack of base
authentication. As such, implementing authentication to some degree could defend against these
types of attacks. A portion of the server’s IP addresses could be reserved for MAC addresses that
have been authenticated after a previous handshake. This would, in theory, eliminate the issue of
real devices being disconnected from the network after their IP address lease ends.
In future work, a more complete IDS will implement detection capabilities for all the
protocols that Yersinia attacks. One such protocol that we currently couldn’t implement
detection for was the Cisco Discovery protocol as it requires cisco manufactured equipment
within the network which we did not have access to. We also would like to work the IDS to
detect VLAN deletion/injection. Testing this IDS through the VM network is restrictive to the
CPU power of the device running the network so we would need to establish the VM network in
a higher CPU device.
